      "transcript": "Elodie Marwick: Welcome to Riverside Produce Auction. Today’s visit follows a one-way route so that the public can see how lots are inspected and sold without entering working areas. Keep your plan in front of you as I describe the site.\nElodie Marwick: From the southern entrance, look to the far left, the north-west corner. The raised public gallery occupies that corner.\nElodie Marwick: North is at the top of the plan. The visitor entrance is centred on the south wall. The western service lane is used by forklifts and delivery vehicles, so it is never part of the public route.\nElodie Marwick: The bidding hall is the large open space in the centre, not the smaller office against the north wall.\nElodie Marwick: Registration confirms the group list and issues a temporary badge. It does not register you as a commercial buyer. Anyone taking part in the short demonstration receives a separate numbered card.\nElodie Marwick: Directly east of the gallery, before you reach the cold rooms, you will see the sample bench.\nElodie Marwick: The gallery gives a clear view of the floor. Please remain behind the rail. Staff may bring representative produce to the sample area, but visitors do not open crates or enter the cold-storage zone.\nElodie Marwick: The insulated rooms grouped along the north-east wall are the cold rooms.\nElodie Marwick: A bidding clock will be demonstrated slowly. In normal trading it moves quickly, yet today the operator pauses it so the guide can explain price changes. The sample transaction is cancelled after the demonstration.\nElodie Marwick: Follow the visitor route down to the south-east corner. The wash point is beside the exit but outside the bidding hall.\nElodie Marwick: Hygiene boundaries matter because unpacked produce is being handled nearby. The public wash point is provided at the end of the route; the staff wash area beyond the line is not available to visitors.\nElodie Marwick: The permit desk is immediately beside the southern entrance, so visitors register before entering the main route.\nElodie Marwick: At departure, badges are returned near the exit. If a visitor has made an authorised purchase, staff attach a collection slip and move the box to the dispatch side. Nobody carries goods back through the gallery.\nElodie Marwick: At that desk, visitors first show photo ID. A booking email alone does not complete registration.\nElodie Marwick: The yellow hygiene line separates the public route from produce handling. Only staff may cross it, even after bidding has ended.\nElodie Marwick: For the bidding explanation, the auctioneer uses a small sample lot, not an entire commercial consignment.\nElodie Marwick: If you buy anything during the supervised session, boxes are collected later from the dispatch lane on the west side.\nElodie Marwick: Visitors often ask whether they can photograph the auction. Wide views from the gallery are allowed during the demonstration, but staff faces, buyer cards and computer screens must not be photographed. The guide will identify a safe point before anyone uses a camera. The cold rooms are visible through windows but remain restricted because doors open onto a working vehicle route. Near the sample bench, labels show produce grade and lot number. They are examples for orientation, not promises about quality. Payment for an authorised visitor purchase is completed at the permit desk after the tour, never on the bidding floor. A collection slip is then matched with the box at dispatch. If the auction alarm sounds, visitors leave by the nearest marked exit and do not retrace the full one-way tour. Guides and staff account for the group outside the southern fence.\nElodie Marwick: We will now walk to the permit desk in pairs. Please keep the central doorway clear for auction staff.",
