      "title": "Listening — Part 3: educational/training discussion",
      "instructions": "21-25: Complete the sentences. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.\n26-30: Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.",
      "transcript": "Merrick Thorneby: Priyanka, our tutor’s main concern was that we had mixed legibility with navigation. A sign may be easy to read yet still produce a poor route choice. We need measures for both the immediate decision and the eventual path.\nPriyanka Shawl: The route should start at the student union. Beginning in the design studio would make the building too familiar to our volunteers.\nMerrick Thorneby: The campus route has four junctions. Two are visually simple and two contain competing doors and noticeboards. If every participant sees the signs in one fixed order, fatigue could be confused with the spacing condition.\nPriyanka Shawl: Across all conditions we keep the same typeface. Otherwise a change attributed to spacing could actually come from letter shape.\nMerrick Thorneby: We should control wording too. Each destination name will contain a similar number of letters, and the arrow size remains constant. Colour contrast and illumination must be checked before the session rather than treated as experimental factors.\nPriyanka Shawl: We will mark a fixed viewing distance on the floor before each choice point, so everyone first sees the sign from the same position.\nMerrick Thorneby: Decision time can be recorded automatically when the participant crosses the floor marker. Completion time covers the whole route, but it includes walking speed, so it belongs in a secondary analysis.\nPriyanka Shawl: Our main outcome should be decision time: the interval from the sign becoming visible to the participant choosing a direction.\nMerrick Thorneby: Participants need a neutral task, such as locating a booked tutorial room. If we tell them the experiment is about typography, they may stare at letter forms instead of navigating naturally.\nPriyanka Shawl: If the participant enters the wrong corridor, we record a route error, even if they notice and correct it immediately.\nMerrick Thorneby: The pilot is intended to expose practical faults. A staff member unfamiliar with this building is suitable because the person can follow instructions but cannot rely on a remembered shortcut.\nPriyanka Shawl: Three sign heights seemed useful, but height would add another changing variable. We need to isolate spacing first.\nMerrick Thorneby: For analysis, each participant sees all spacing levels, but the order is counterbalanced. We will also note corrected errors separately so that a brief hesitation does not count as a successful direct route.\nPriyanka Shawl: Let us use three predefined spacing levels: standard, moderately open and very open. We should not adjust them for individuals.\nMerrick Thorneby: After the pilot, we can decide whether a larger study needs eye tracking. For now, a camera at the junction and a timing sensor are sufficient and less intrusive.\nPriyanka Shawl: For the pilot, recruit staff who are unfamiliar with the building. Design students may recognise our manipulation, and library users know the route.\nMerrick Thorneby: The camera should frame the choice point and record movement. We do not need faces or a recording of anyone reading aloud.\nPriyanka Shawl: I will draft the consent form, while Merrick prepares the randomised sign sequence so the conditions are not always presented in the same order.\nMerrick Thorneby: We should decide how many practice junctions participants receive. One unscored example can confirm that they understand the task and the timing marker, but it must use a sign not repeated in the main route. Too much practice would make the study about learning our system. At every junction, the destination wording should remain visible for the same duration. If a participant walks back to reread a sign, the timer continues and the observer records a reconsideration. We must distinguish that from a route error, which requires entering the incorrect corridor. The camera files will be named with participant codes and junction numbers. Audio will not be collected, because speech is irrelevant to the hypothesis and would add personal data. After the route, participants can comment on comfort and clarity, but those opinions should not replace behavioural measures. A person may prefer wide spacing even if it produces slower decisions. The analysis can report both outcomes without assuming they must agree.\nPriyanka Shawl: That leaves the information sheet and risk assessment. We can divide those after Merrick has produced the sign sequence.",
