      "instructions": "31-35: Complete the sentences. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.\n36-40: Complete the notes. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.",
      "transcript": "Lecturer: Booming dunes are memorable because a familiar material produces an unfamiliar acoustic effect. The sound is not made by wind whistling across the crest. It accompanies the collective motion of sand on a steep slip face.\nLecturer: The event begins when dry sand avalanches down the steep avalanche face of a dune, whether the movement starts naturally or is triggered carefully.\nLecturer: Not every dune booms. Shape alone is insufficient, and even a known booming dune may remain quiet after rain. Researchers therefore examine the material, the recent weather and the way an avalanche is initiated.\nLecturer: Booming material often contains grains within a narrow range of grain sizes; a broad mixture does not necessarily move in the same coordinated way.\nLecturer: Individual grain collisions produce brief impulses, but the audible tone persists. The central scientific question is how many moving grains become sufficiently coordinated for energy to concentrate around one frequency.\nLecturer: Instead of a sharp crack, observers hear a sustained tone with a relatively low frequency, sometimes lasting while the avalanche continues.\nLecturer: One family of explanations emphasizes synchronization within the flowing layer. Another gives a larger role to a subsurface layer that could amplify or select vibrations. Field evidence has not removed every disagreement between these models.\nLecturer: Dryness matters. Too much moisture between grains can prevent the sound even when the slope and movement look suitable.\nLecturer: Careful measurement separates description from mechanism. Researchers map the slope, record humidity and temperature, and take small samples without assuming that one convenient correlation establishes a cause.\nLecturer: One proposed explanation involves a resonant layer beneath the surface, although its exact role remains debated and should not be presented as settled.\nLecturer: Acoustic data include frequency, amplitude and duration. Vibration sensors add information that a microphone in the air cannot provide. High-speed images can estimate flow speed, but they do not directly reveal forces between grains.\nLecturer: In a field survey, the team first records the slope angle at the active face before disturbing the sand.\nLecturer: Laboratory experiments allow grain properties to be controlled, though a small box cannot reproduce the full geometry of a dune. Field observations preserve scale but introduce changing wind, moisture and surface history.\nLecturer: A calibrated microphone records the sound frequency and its change through time rather than relying on the listener’s description alone.\nLecturer: The best interpretation therefore combines settings. If a model explains only one artificial avalanche, it is incomplete. If it predicts when sound will stop after moisture changes, it becomes more persuasive, but still requires replication.\nLecturer: Collected grains are tested for size and roundness, since similar-looking sands may differ in surface form.\nLecturer: Small buried sensors detect vibration beneath the moving surface and show whether oscillation extends into deeper material.\nLecturer: Finally, competing models are compared with field measurements. A plausible mechanism must explain observations at more than one dune.\nLecturer: Booming is also distinct from the brief squeak produced when a small handful of sand is rubbed or pushed. The field phenomenon involves a much larger moving mass and can be heard at a considerable distance. That difference in scale is one reason a laboratory imitation remains difficult. Grain surfaces change through transport. Wind sorts particles and repeated collisions can alter their shape or coating. Researchers compare booming and silent sands, yet a sample removed from its dune may behave differently once humidity and packing change. The history of the surface matters too. A recent avalanche can expose material with different temperature or moisture from the uppermost layer. Repeating a test at the same location is therefore not necessarily an identical replication. To investigate synchronization, teams compare the tone with the motion of grains and subsurface vibration. A stable frequency during changing flow speed poses a different challenge to a model than a frequency that tracks speed closely. Uncertainty should be retained in the conclusion. A resonant layer is an important hypothesis, not a hidden instrument that has been directly observed at every booming dune. Likewise, similar grain size is often associated with booming but does not guarantee it. The combined evidence points to collective motion under restricted material and environmental conditions. Future work benefits from standard reporting of weather, slope, sampling depth and acoustic calibration, so apparently conflicting observations can be compared rather than treated as simple contradictions. Comparable records also reveal whether the same mechanism remains plausible across seasons and sites.\nLecturer: The broader lesson is methodological: striking sounds invite simple stories, whereas the evidence demands measurements across grains, flow and the dune body.",
