      "passage": "A. Sound is usually treated as information, but an acoustic field also carries energy and can exert force. Under suitable conditions, that force can counter gravity on a very small object. The result is acoustic levitation: contactless suspension rather than a trick in which the object has no support at all. The support is supplied by pressure variations in the sound field. Because the object avoids a solid holder, researchers are interested in handling droplets or delicate samples that might be contaminated, distorted or absorbed by a surface.\n\nB. The simplest arrangements create a standing wave between an emitter and a reflecting surface. At particular positions, the forces can balance and form a stable trap. This geometry is conceptually clear, but the reflector restricts access and the trap positions are largely fixed by the wavelength. A droplet that grows or changes shape may respond differently. The method is therefore useful for demonstrating the principle and studying stationary samples, but less suited to complex paths.\n\nC. A phased array replaces one emitter with many controllable elements. By changing the relative timing of their signals, the system can reshape the field and move a pressure trap without physically moving the whole apparatus. This flexibility permits steering, although control becomes computationally demanding. The array must account for interactions between sound, object and surroundings. A command that moves one sample smoothly may destabilise another with a different size or density.\n\nD. Open-loop control assumes that the object follows the predicted path. Feedback control checks that assumption. A camera tracks the object, software compares its actual and intended positions, and the emitters are adjusted. This can correct small disturbances, but the measurement introduces delay. If correction arrives too late, an aggressive response may amplify the error. The design challenge is therefore not merely detecting motion; it is making a timely adjustment that restores stability without creating oscillation.\n\nE. Another approach modifies the space around the acoustic field. A shaped chamber can guide or concentrate pressure while reducing the number of independently controlled emitters. It may offer efficient suspension for a narrow class of tasks, but it sacrifices some freedom of access and movement. This illustrates a recurring trade-off: hardware geometry can perform part of the control work, whereas a more open arrangement transfers that burden to sensing and computation.\n\nF. Contactless handling does not make the sample passive. A liquid droplet may evaporate, changing mass and size while it is suspended. Acoustic energy can also affect internal circulation or surface shape. Researchers must therefore distinguish the behaviour they wish to observe from behaviour caused by the holding method. A non-contact technique removes contamination from a clamp or dish, yet introduces its own environment. “Contactless” should not be read as “influence-free”.\n\nG. Practical systems need a control margin: enough reserve force to tolerate modest disturbance without damaging the sample or requiring extreme sound levels. They also need verification, because a stable demonstration does not prove reliable transfer over repeated cycles. The best architecture depends on the task. A stationary measurement may favour a simple standing wave; an irregular route may justify an array and feedback; a high-throughput operation may prefer constrained geometry. Acoustic levitation is therefore a family of design choices, not a single universal device.\n\nH. Handling several objects introduces problems that are absent from a single-sample demonstration. Traps may interact, and moving one object can disturb another. Control software must decide whether to optimise every path simultaneously or sequence movements to preserve a wider safety margin. Verification also becomes harder because a successful average can conceal occasional loss of a fragile sample. For this reason, researchers distinguish proof of principle from an operational protocol. The former shows that force can be generated; the latter specifies repeatability, allowable sample variation and recovery when a trap fails. That distinction is central to responsible claims about practical application. Reporting should include failures as well as the smoothest demonstration. A video of one successful transfer cannot reveal how often a sample escaped, how long recalibration took or which objects were excluded before filming. Repeated trials and clear eligibility ranges turn spectacle into evidence.",
