      "part": 7,
      "skill": "reading",
      "title": "Academic Reading — Passage 3: A fair chance when there are too few places",
      "passageTitle": "A fair chance when there are too few places",
      "sectionStyle": "reading",
      "instructions": "Read the passage and answer the questions. Follow the instruction and word limit shown for each item.",
      "passage": "A. When a programme has fewer places than qualified applicants, selection cannot be solved by checking eligibility alone. Every remaining candidate may satisfy the threshold, yet some rule must still decide who enters. A lottery makes chance explicit. Critics see this as surrendering judgement; supporters argue that it can be more honest than pretending to measure tiny differences precisely. Its legitimacy depends on the stage at which it is used. Random admission before basic screening raises a different issue from random selection among candidates already judged suitable.\n\nB. First-come, first-served allocation appears neutral because everyone faces the same clock. In practice, it rewards access to time, information and reliable technology. People who learn about an opportunity late, work during registration hours or have unstable internet begin at a disadvantage unrelated to the programme’s purpose. A queue can still be appropriate when early action is itself relevant, but convenience should not be mistaken for fairness. The central question is whether speed of application is a quality the institution intends to reward.\n\nC. Merit ranking seems to offer the opposite virtue: places go to those judged strongest. The difficulty comes near the cut-off, where small score differences may reflect measurement noise, unequal preparation or subjective assessment. A detailed rank can project false precision. This does not invalidate evaluation. It suggests that a threshold followed by a lottery may be preferable when the institution can distinguish acceptable from unacceptable candidates more confidently than it can order every acceptable candidate.\n\nD. Lottery design ranges from a complete draw to a tie-breaker used only among equally ranked candidates. Weighted lotteries give additional chances to groups or applicants with specified characteristics. Weighting can pursue access goals while preserving uncertainty, but it also makes the system harder to explain. If weights are poorly justified, randomisation may conceal rather than resolve disagreement. A draw is therefore not automatically fair; its rules encode values about who enters the pool and whether all entries count alike.\n\nE. Trust depends on a published procedure. Applicants should know eligibility rules, the number of available places, whether priority categories exist and how the draw will be checked. Releasing only the final names is insufficient because observers cannot distinguish an honest random process from an arbitrary decision. Independent verification can protect both winners and administrators. Transparency does not remove disappointment, but it changes the basis on which the outcome can be challenged.\n\nF. A lottery also changes what evaluation can reveal. Because selection among eligible candidates is random, later differences between selected and unselected groups may help researchers study programme effects, provided ethical and methodological conditions are met. Yet evaluation should not become the primary justification for denying a place. The allocation rule must first be defensible to applicants. A post-allocation review should also test whether eligibility and weighting created unintended exclusion.\n\nG. No allocation method is universally fair. Queues privilege prompt access, rankings privilege chosen measures, and lotteries privilege an equal or deliberately weighted chance. The appropriate rule follows from institutional purpose and from the kinds of error decision-makers are willing to accept. Where differences in merit are substantial and reliably measured, ranking has a strong claim. Where acceptable candidates are numerous and finely ranked differences are doubtful, randomisation may acknowledge uncertainty more honestly.\n\nH. The strongest case is for bounded randomisation: judgement defines a defensible pool, and chance resolves scarcity inside it. This model neither eliminates expertise nor treats applicants as identical. It asks experts to make the distinction they can support, then prevents them from inventing confidence beyond that point. Such restraint may feel less decisive than a complete ranking, but decisiveness is not the same as accuracy. A legitimate lottery is not an escape from reasons; it is a rule for acting when reasons run out.\n\nI. In every allocation round, applicants judge an allocation system not only by their personal outcome but also by whether the procedure treats them with dignity. A transparent draw can still feel dismissive if the institution offers no explanation or appeal for an eligibility error. Conversely, a carefully communicated lottery may be accepted even by people who lose, because the remaining scarcity is acknowledged openly. Communication cannot rescue an unjust pool, but it can expose mistakes before randomisation and clarify what chance did and did not decide. Procedural dignity therefore adds another requirement: people must be able to understand the rule and correct factual errors that wrongly exclude them.",
